    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A 1,500-watt generator runs about 1,000 to 1,200 watts, which keeps a refrigerator, lights, phones and a laptop going and weighs around 30 pounds. It will not run a space heater, a microwave or a well pump. Buy one for light loads and portability, and buy a bigger generator or a second unit if you need more.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="what-the-class-is" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What a 1,500-Watt Generator Really Is
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the number on the box. Almost every small generator is sold by its peak, or surge, rating, which is the most it can deliver for a few seconds while a motor starts. The number that runs your appliances is the running, or rated, watts, and in this class it is typically 200 to 500 watts lower. A generator sold as 1,500 watts usually runs at 1,000 to 1,200; one sold as 1,200 watts usually runs at 900 to 1,000. Every comparison on this page uses running watts, because that is the figure that decides what you can plug in.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is the smallest useful class of gasoline generator, sitting below the 2,000 to 2,500-watt inverters that most campers and RV owners buy. The difference is not subtle. Our <a href="/best-2000-watt-inverter-generator/">2000 watt inverter generator guide</a> covers units that run 1,600 to 2,200 watts and weigh 39 to 50 pounds; the generators here run 900 to 1,200 watts and weigh roughly 27 to 35 pounds. If you need to run a microwave or a 30-amp RV, buy from that class. If you need something light that keeps a fridge, phones and lights going, this is the class to look at.
          </p>

          <p>
            Nearly everything worth buying in this class is an inverter generator. An inverter converts the engine's output to direct current and then back to clean alternating current, with total harmonic distortion low enough for laptops, phones, TVs and CPAP machines, and it lets the engine throttle down at light loads, which saves fuel and noise. The alternative at this size is the open-frame conventional generator, often two-stroke, which is cheaper, louder and produces rougher power. Our guide to <a href="/inverter-generator-vs-regular-generator/">inverter vs regular generators</a> explains why the price gap is worth paying for anything with a circuit board.
          </p>

          <p>
            Who actually buys this class? Three groups, mostly. Tailgaters and campers who want something light enough to lift into a truck bed with one hand. Apartment and condo owners who want a cheap way to keep a fridge running during an outage and have no room for a big generator. And owners of battery power stations who want a small, cheap way to recharge one outdoors during a long outage. The picks below are chosen with all three in mind.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-it-runs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What 1,000 to 1,200 Running Watts Will Run
          </h2>

          <p>
            A 1,000-watt generator runs more than most people expect, provided the loads are small and you do not run them all at once. The table below shows typical running and starting draws for common loads; check the label on your own appliances, because models vary widely. Our <a href="/generator-wattage-chart/">generator wattage chart</a> has a longer list.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Load</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical running watts</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">On 1,000W?</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">On 1,200W?</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Phone, laptop, router</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10-100W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">LED lights (several rooms)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">40-100W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">CPAP (no heated humidifier)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30-60W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Full-size refrigerator</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">100-200W, higher at startup</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">TV and streaming box</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">60-200W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">5,000 BTU window AC</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 400-500W, higher at startup</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Marginal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually, on its own</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Coffee maker</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">600-1,200W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Small ones, alone</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Most, alone</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1,000W microwave</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1,500W from the wall</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1,500W space heater</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,500W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The refrigerator is the load that decides most purchases. A typical full-size fridge runs at 100 to 200 watts but draws a startup surge several times higher each time the compressor kicks in. A 1,250 to 1,500-watt surge rating handles most modern refrigerators, which is why this class is a workable outage generator for an apartment. Older fridges and large side-by-sides with ice makers can be harder to start, so if you have one, check its label or measure it with a plug-in watt meter before relying on a 1,000-watt unit.
          </p>

          <p>
            The microwave is where buyers get caught. A microwave sold as 1,000 watts is rated by its cooking output, and draws roughly 1,400 to 1,600 watts from the outlet. That is more than any generator here can run continuously. The same goes for most hair dryers, toasters and kettles, which sit at 1,200 to 1,800 watts. If you need those, move up to the 2,000-watt class or add a second unit in parallel.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical way to use one of these generators is to run one big load at a time. Keep the fridge, the lights and the phones on continuously, and treat the coffee maker or the window AC as something you switch on once the fridge has stopped cycling. Do the sums on running watts, then add the single largest startup surge, and compare that total with the generator's running rating, not its peak.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="space-heater" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Space Heaters, Well Pumps and Other Things It Won't Do
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most common question about small generators in winter is whether one can run a space heater. On its own, a standard 1,500-watt heater on its high setting: no. That is 12.5 amps continuously, which exceeds the running rating of every generator on this page. The generator will either trip its overload protection within seconds or run at its limit until it does. A heater with a 750-watt low setting will run on a 1,000 or 1,200-watt unit, but at that load runtime falls to a few hours per tank and nothing else can share the outlet.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is also an inefficient use of fuel. Burning gasoline in a small engine to make electricity to make heat throws away most of the energy along the way. If heat is the main outage problem, an indoor-rated heater designed for the purpose makes more sense, and our guide to <a href="/best-indoor-heater-for-power-outage/">indoor heaters for power outages</a> covers the safe options and the carbon monoxide rules that come with them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Well pumps are out too. Most residential well pumps are 240V, and every generator in this class is 120V only; even the 120V pumps need more starting current than a 1,000-watt unit can supply. The same applies to central air, electric water heaters, electric ranges and dryers. None of these loads are realistic below the 3,500 to 5,000-watt range, and many need 240V. If any of them is on your must-run list, start with our <a href="/best-portable-generator/">portable generator guide</a> rather than this one.
          </p>

          <p>
            A gas furnace blower is the borderline case. Many furnace blowers run at 400 to 800 watts, which is within reach of a 1,200-watt unit, but a furnace is hard-wired and needs a proper transfer switch or a furnace-specific inlet to connect safely. If you plan to run one, size the generator to the blower's nameplate current, not a guess, and have an electrician wire the connection. Never backfeed a wall outlet with a double-ended cord to make it work.
          </p>
        </div>

