              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 1,500-watt heater on high draws more than any generator in this class can supply continuously. It will trip the overload or run at its limit. Use a heater's low setting with nothing else connected, or choose an indoor-rated heater designed for outages.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Plugging electronics into a two-stroke</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Conventional open-frame generators, including cheap two-strokes, produce rougher power than inverters. Laptops, CPAP machines, TVs and phone chargers belong on an inverter. Save the conventional unit for work lights and simple tools.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running it in a garage or on a balcony</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Small engines still produce carbon monoxide. The CDC and CPSC say never run a generator in a garage, basement, crawlspace or enclosed porch, and keep it 20 feet from windows and doors. Balconies and carports put exhaust right where it gets into the home.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a thin, long extension cord</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A light-duty indoor cord drops voltage and can overheat at 1,000 watts. Use an outdoor-rated 14-gauge cord at a minimum for 25 to 50 feet, 12-gauge for longer runs or a refrigerator, and keep one cord per major appliance where you can.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

